q(D,C,9,"What is the 'extra Calvinisticum'?",["A Calvinist extra","The Son of God existed beyond (extra) His human nature even during the incarnation","An extra Calvinist belief","A type of predestination"],1,"Extra Calvinisticum: the Logos was not confined to His human nature but continued to sustain the universe during the incarnation.","Colossians 1:17","Colossians","Christology"),
q(D,C,10,"What is the 'Mia hypostasis' (one person) Christology?",["One nature","Christ is one person (hypostasis) with two natures (divine and human)","One will","One substance"],1,"Chalcedonian Christology: Christ is one person (hypostasis) in two natures, without confusion, change, division, or separation.","Hebrews 1:3","Hebrews","Christology"),
q(D,C,11,"What was the Council of Chalcedon (451 AD)?",["A church council on baptism","The council that defined Christ as one person in two natures (fully God and fully man)","A council on the Trinity","A council on justification"],1,"Chalcedon (451 AD) defined the hypostatic union: Christ is one person in two natures, without mixing.",N,N,"Church History"),
q(D,C,12,"What was the Council of Nicaea (325 AD)?",["A council on baptism","The council that affirmed Christ as 'of the same substance' (homoousios) as the Father","A council on the canon","A council on eschatology"],1,"Nicaea (325 AD) affirmed Christ's full deity: He is 'of the same substance' (homoousios) as the Father.","John 1:1","John","Church History"),
q(D,C,13,"What was the Arian controversy?",["A debate about Arius' music","Arius taught Christ was a created being, not co-eternal with the Father","A debate about church government","A debate about baptism"],1,"Arius taught that Christ (the Logos) was the first created being, not God — condemned at Nicaea.","John 1:1","John","Church History"),
q(D,C,14,"What was the Pelagian controversy?",["A debate about Pelagius' writings","Pelagius taught humans could achieve salvation by their own efforts without grace","A debate about the Trinity","A debate about baptism"],1,"Pelagius denied original sin and taught salvation by works — Augustine opposed him.","Romans 5:12","Romans","Church History"),
q(D,C,15,"What was the Nestorian controversy?",["A debate about Nestorius' theology","Nestorius separated Christ's two natures so much that He was virtually two persons","A debate about baptism","A debate about the Spirit"],1,"Nestorius so emphasized Christ's two natures that he effectively made Him two persons — condemned at Ephesus (431).","John 1:14","John","Church History"),
q(D,C,16,"What was the Monophysite controversy?",["A debate about monks","Monophysitism taught Christ had only one nature (divine), absorbing the human","A debate about one will","A debate about monotheism"],1,"Monophysitism (Eutyches) taught Christ's divine nature absorbed His human nature — condemned at Chalcedon (451).","John 1:14","John","Church History"),
q(D,C,17,"What is 'Monothelitism'?",["One God","The heresy that Christ had only one will (divine), rather than two wills (divine and human)","One nature","One baptism"],1,"Monothelitism was condemned at Constantinople III (680): Christ has two wills (divine and human) in one person.","Luke 22:42","Luke","Christology"),
q(D,C,18,"What is 'Docetism'?",["A writing style","The heresy that Christ only 'seemed' (dokeo) to have a physical body","A type of doctrine","A Greek philosophy"],1,"Docetism (from dokeo, 'to seem') denied Christ's true humanity — He only appeared human.","1 John 4:2","1 John","Christology"),
q(D,C,19,"What is 'Gnosticism'?",["A type of knowledge","A heresy teaching salvation through secret knowledge (gnosis) and that matter is evil","A type of mysticism","A philosophical school"],1,"Gnosticism taught salvation through secret knowledge and that the material world was created by a lesser being.","Colossians 2:8","Colossians","Heresy"),
q(D,C,20,"What is ' Adoptionism'?",["Adopting children","The heresy that Jesus was a normal human whom God 'adopted' as His Son at baptism","A type of parenting","A type of baptism"],1,"Adoptionism taught Jesus was a mere man adopted by God at His baptism — denied Christ's eternal sonship.","Mark 1:11","Mark","Christology"),
q(D,C,21,"What is the difference between 'supralapsarianism' and 'infralapsarianism'?",["They are the same","Supra: election logically precedes the decree to permit the fall; Infra: election logically follows the decree to permit the fall","Supra is Calvinist; Infra is Arminian","They are both Arminian"],1,"Supralapsarianism: election precedes the fall decree. Infralapsarianism: election follows the fall decree. Both are Reformed.","Romans 9:11","Romans","Theology"),
q(D,C,22,"What is the 'ordo salutis' (order of salvation)?",["A list of Bible books","The logical sequence of salvation: calling, regeneration, faith, justification, adoption, sanctification, glorification","A type of prayer","A church calendar"],1,"The ordo salutis describes the logical order of God's saving work in a believer's life.","Romans 8:30","Romans","Theology"),
q(D,C,23,"What is 'regeneration' in Reformed theology?",["Being reborn physically","The Holy Spirit's sovereign act of giving spiritual life to the spiritually dead, preceding faith","Being baptized","Going to church"],1,"Regeneration precedes faith in Reformed theology — the Spirit gives new life, then the person believes.","John 3:3","John","Theology"),
q(D,C,24,"What is the difference between 'monergism' and 'synergism'?",["They are the same","Monergism: God alone saves (Reformed); Synergism: God and human cooperate (Arminian/Catholic)","Monergism is Catholic; synergism is Protestant","They are both Catholic"],1,"Monergism (Reformed): God alone acts in regeneration. Synergism (Arminian/Catholic): God and human cooperate.","Ephesians 2:8","Ephesians","Theology"),
q(D,C,25,"What is the 'perspicuity of Scripture'?",["Scripture is unclear","Scripture is sufficiently clear that ordinary believers can understand its essential teachings","Scripture is for scholars only","Scripture needs tradition"],1,"Perspicuity means the Bible is clear enough that ordinary believers can understand the essentials of salvation without an infallible interpreter.","Psalm 119:105","Psalms","Scripture"),
q(D,C,26,"What is the difference between 'inerrancy' and 'infallibility'?",["They are the same","Inerrancy: the Bible is without error in all it affirms; Infallibility: the Bible will not fail in its purpose","Inerrancy is Catholic; infallibility is Protestant","They are both wrong"],1,"Inerrancy = no errors in what the Bible affirms. Infallibility = the Bible reliably accomplishes its purpose.","2 Timothy 3:16","2 Timothy","Scripture"),
q(D,C,27,"What is 'sensus plenior' (fuller sense)?",["A type of meaning","A deeper, God-intended meaning in a text that the human author may not have fully understood","A type of translation","A hermeneutical error"],1,"Sensus plenior: a deeper meaning intended by God but not fully grasped by the human author (e.g., Isaiah 53).","1 Peter 1:10-11","1 Peter","Hermeneutics"),
q(D,C,28,"What is the 'Mosaic law' divided into?",["Two parts","Three parts: moral, civil, and ceremonial","Four parts: moral, civil, ceremonial, dietary","One part: all moral"],1,"The Mosaic law is commonly divided into moral (Ten Commandments), civil (case laws), and ceremonial (sacrifices, feasts).","Exodus 20","Exodus","Law"),
q(D,C,29,"What is the 'third use of the law' (tertius usus legis)?",["The law as punishment","The law as a guide for the Christian life (moral law still applies to believers)","The law as civil code","The law as ceremony"],1,"The third use of the law: the moral law continues to guide believers in sanctification (Reformed view).","Romans 3:31","Romans","Law"),
q(D,C,30,"What is the 'covenant of redemption' (pactum salutis)?",["A human covenant","The eternal intra-Trinitarian covenant: the Father elects, the Son redeems, the Spirit applies salvation","The New covenant","The Abrahamic covenant"],1,"The pactum salutis: the Father gives the Son a people; the Son redeems them; the Spirit applies redemption.","Ephesians 1:4","Ephesians","Theology"),
q(D,C,31,"What is 'definite atonement' (limited atonement)?",["Christ died for no one","Christ's atonement was specifically for the elect, accomplishing their salvation","Christ's atonement was limited in power","Christ's atonement was for angels only"],1,"Definite atonement: Christ's death actually secures salvation for the elect, not merely making salvation possible.","John 10:11","John","Theology"),
q(D,C,32,"What is the difference between 'limited atonement' and 'unlimited atonement'?",["They are the same","Limited: Christ died specifically for the elect; Unlimited: Christ died for all people","Limited is Arminian; Unlimited is Calvinist","They both deny the cross"],1,"Calvinism: limited/definite atonement (for the elect). Arminianism: unlimited atonement (for all, effective for believers).","1 John 2:2","1 John","Theology"),
q(D,C,33,"What is 'common grace'?",["Grace for salvation","God's goodness to all people, including unbelievers (rain, sun, morality, culture)","Grace given to all for salvation","Grace given to the elect"],1,"Common grace: God's non-saving goodness to all (Matthew 5:45 — He causes his sun to rise on the evil and the good).","Matthew 5:45","Matthew","Theology"),
q(D,C,34,"What is 'special grace'?",["Uncommon grace","God's saving grace given to the elect, resulting in salvation","Grace for special occasions","Grace for church leaders"],1,"Special grace: God's saving grace that effectually brings the elect to faith and salvation.","Ephesians 2:8","Ephesians","Theology"),
q(D,C,35,"What is the 'image of God' (imago Dei) in theology?",["A picture","The likeness to God in humans: rational, moral, relational, and spiritual faculties","A statue","A reflection"],1,"The imago Dei includes rational, moral, relational, and spiritual capacities that distinguish humans from animals.","Genesis 1:26","Genesis","Theology"),
q(D,C,36,"What are the main views on the imago Dei?",["One view","Relational, substantive, and functional views","Structural and dynamic only","Material and spiritual only"],1,"Three main views: relational (in relationship), substantive (in our nature/ faculties), functional (in our role/vocation).","Genesis 1:26","Genesis","Theology"),
q(D,C,37,"What is the 'cultural mandate'?",["To create culture","God's command to fill, subdue, and steward the earth (Genesis 1:28)","A mandate to evangelize","A mandate to build the temple"],1,"Genesis 1:28 — God blessed them and said: Fill the earth and subdue it; have dominion.","Genesis 1:28","Genesis","Theology"),
q(D,C,38,"What is the difference between 'soul' and 'spirit' in biblical anthropology?",["They are completely different","Some see them as synonymous (dichotomist); others distinguish them (trichotomist)","Soul is good; spirit is bad","Soul is physical; spirit is spiritual"],1,"Dichotomists see soul and spirit as the same immaterial aspect; trichotomists distinguish them.","1 Thessalonians 5:23","1 Thessalonians","Theology"),
q(D,C,39,"What is 'dichotomy' vs. 'trichotomy' in anthropology?",["Two vs. three persons","Dichotomy: humans are body and soul/spirit (two parts); Trichotomy: body, soul, and spirit (three parts)","Two vs. three natures","Two vs. three covenants"],1,"Dichotomists: body + soul/spirit. Trichotomists: body + soul + spirit. Most Reformed theologians are dichotomists.","Matthew 10:28","Matthew","Theology"),
q(D,C,40,"What is 'traducianism' vs. 'creationism' regarding the soul?",["Two views on creation","Traducianism: souls are inherited from parents; Creationism: God creates each soul individually","Two views on baptism","Two views on eschatology"],1,"Traducianism: the soul is transmitted through natural generation. Creationism: God creates each soul individually at conception.","Psalm 51:5","Psalms","Theology"),
q(D,C,41,"What is the 'communicable' vs. 'incommunicable' attributes of God?",["Attributes that can/cannot be shared","Communicable: attributes humans can reflect (love, justice); Incommunicable: unique to God (omnipresence, eternality)","Attributes that are spoken/unspoken","Attributes that are old/new"],1,"Communicable attributes (love, justice, wisdom) can be reflected in humans; incommunicable (omnipresence, eternity, omnipotence) cannot.","Genesis 1:27","Genesis","Theology"),
q(D,C,42,"What is 'divine simplicity'?",["God is simple to understand","God is not composed of parts; His attributes are identical with His essence","God is simple-minded","God is one person"],1,"Divine simplicity: God is not composed of parts. His love IS His justice IS His essence.","Deuteronomy 6:4","Deuteronomy","Theology"),
q(D,C,43,"What is 'divine impassibility'?",["God is passable","God is not subject to involuntary emotions or suffering; He is perfectly blessed","God cannot feel","God suffers with us"],1,"Divine impassibility: God is not overwhelmed or controlled by emotions; He is perfectly fulfilled in Himself.","Malachi 3:6","Malachi","Theology"),
q(D,C,44,"What is 'divine aseity'?",["God is self-existent","God exists from Himself (a se), not dependent on anything","God is self-revealing","God is self-limiting"],1,"Aseity (from a se): God is self-existent and independent — He depends on nothing for His existence.","Exodus 3:14","Exodus","Theology"),
q(D,C,45,"What is 'divine impassibility' in classical theism?",["God suffers","God is not subject to involuntary passions or suffering; He is perfectly blessed","God is emotional","God is indifferent"],1,"Impassibility: God is not overwhelmed by involuntary emotions. He freely loves, but is not controlled by passion.","Psalm 102:27","Psalms","Theology"),
q(D,C,46,"What is the difference between 'classical theism' and 'theistic personalism'?",["They are the same","Classical: God is simple, impassible, timeless; Personalism: God is more relational, emotional, temporal","Classical is older; personalism is newer","They are both wrong"],1,"Classical theism (Aquinas): God is simple, impassible, timeless. Theistic personalism: God is more relational and temporal.","Psalm 90:2","Psalms","Theology"),
q(D,C,47,"What is 'open theism'?",["God is open","The view that God does not exhaustively know future free choices — He is 'open' to the future","God is approachable","God is transparent"],1,"Open theism: God does not know future free decisions; the future is 'open' even to God — rejected by classical theism.","Isaiah 46:10","Isaiah","Theology"),
q(D,C,48,"What is 'middle knowledge' (Molinism)?",["Average knowledge","God's knowledge of what free creatures would do in any possible circumstance (between natural and free knowledge)","Knowledge between OT and NT","A type of wisdom"],1,"Molinism (Luis de Molina): God knows 'counterfactuals of freedom' — what any person would freely do in any situation.","1 Samuel 23:12","1 Samuel","Theology"),
q(D,C,49,"What is 'theodicy'?",["A theocracy","The justification of God's goodness in the face of evil and suffering","A type of theology","A defense of the Bible"],1,"Theodicy (from theos + dike): the attempt to justify God's goodness and power despite the existence of evil.","Job 38:1","Job","Theology"),
q(D,C,50,"What is the 'free will defense' (Alvin Plantinga)?",["A legal defense","The argument that God cannot actualize a world with genuinely free creatures who always choose good","A defense of free will","A type of apologetics"],1,"Plantinga's Free Will Defense: it's possible that God couldn't create a world with free creatures who never sin — evil is a necessary possibility of free will.","Genesis 2:16","Genesis","Theology"),
